ben: Drafting the Go sample now. Will keep it intentionally minimal and explicit about status checks:
```go
package main

import (
  "context"
  "fmt"
  "time"

  "github.com/redwoodai/redwood-go"
)

func main(){
  client := redwood.New(redwood.Config{APIKey: "${REDWOOD_API_KEY}"})
  for attempt:=0; attempt<4; attempt++ {
    resp, err := client.Generate(context.Background(), redwood.GenerateParams{Prompt: "Summarize: ...", MaxTokens: 50})
    if err == nil {
      fmt.Println(resp.Text)
      return
    }
    if redwood.IsStatus(err, 429) && attempt < 3 {
      sleep := time.Duration((1<<attempt))*time.Second + time.Duration(rand.Intn(250))*time.Millisecond
      time.Sleep(sleep)
      continue
    }
    panic(err)
  }
}
```

sophie: Nice. Can we also surface a one-paragraph observability tip? e.g., call out how to distinguish bursty 429s from sustained capacity issues using token-level latency percentiles and queue depth in the Console. A short recommended dashboard widget list would help.

maria: Yes. Add a small "How to tell the difference" note: "Burst: short-lived 429 spike with p95 latency stable -> increase client-side jitter/backoff or prefer regional variants. Sustained: rising p95/p99 + queue growth -> consider increasing dedicated capacity, batching config, or KV-cache retention."

ben: Pushed Go snippet update and added an explicit comment about avoiding implicit retries. Also included a tiny TS example using unified SDK v2 (kept minimal).

ben: TS snippet:
```ts
import { Redwood } from "@redwood/sdk"
const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY })
async function gen(){
  for(let i=0;i<3;i++){
    try{
      const r = await client.generate({ prompt: "Summarize...", max_tokens:50 })
      console.log(r.text)
      break
    }catch(e:any){
      if(e.status === 429 && i<2) await new Promise(r=>setTimeout(r, Math.pow(2,i)*1000 + Math.random()*200))
      else throw e
    }
  }
}
```

